====Usage notes====
* In Early Modern English, the second-person informal singular indicative verb forms used with {{m|en|thou}} were {{m|en|becomest}} in the present and {{m|en|becamest}} in the past tenses. Similarly, {{m|en|becometh}} was used as a third-person singular indicative present form.
* Also in Early Modern English, {{m|en||become}} (and certain other intransitive verbs, such as  {{m|en|come}} and {{m|en|go}}) used the auxiliary {{m|en|be}} rather than {{m|en|have}} for perfect aspect constructions. In current usage, {{m|en|have}} is standard, though ''be'' is still occasionally used poetically.
*: {{quote-av|en|year=1965|title=The Decision to Drop the Bomb|medium=documentary|speaker=w:J. Robert Oppenheimer|url=https://archive.org/details/90984-the-decision-to-drop-the-bomb-vwr|archivedate=2021-03-25 21:29:25|origlang=sa|origtext=sa:कालोऽस्मि लोकक्षयकृत्प्रवृद्धो|origtr=-|text=I remembered the line from the Hindu scripture, the ''Bhagavad-Gita'': Vishnu is trying to persuade the Prince that he should do his duty and, to impress him, he takes on his multi-armed form and says, "Now I '''am''' become Death, the destroyer of worlds." I suppose we all thought that, one way or another.}}

